    dict(id='TN06-F3', mid='06', section='toxicity', stem='transplant-nurse-06-tacrolimus-narrow-window-toxicity', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='Tacrolimus: protecting the kidney inside a narrow window', content='Tacrolimus narrow therapeutic window (rejection below, toxicity above) and an organ map of calcineurin-inhibitor toxicities with their mechanisms', alt='Therapeutic window band for tacrolimus with rejection below and toxicity above, and a body map of its side effects including afferent arteriole narrowing in the kidney, high potassium, low magnesium, tremor, high blood pressure and new diabetes.', desc="Tacrolimus works only inside a narrow, moving window: too little invites rejection, too much constricts the kidney's own arterioles and causes a predictable list of side effects.", abbrevs=[]),
    dict(id='TN06-F4', mid='06', section='interactions', stem='transplant-nurse-06-cyp3a-pgp-interaction-seesaw', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='What pushes tacrolimus up — and what pulls it down', content='How CYP3A and P-glycoprotein in the gut wall and liver control tacrolimus exposure, with Philippine-relevant inhibitors and inducers', alt='Gut wall and liver illustration of CYP3A enzymes and P-glycoprotein handling tacrolimus, beside a gauge showing drugs and foods that raise levels, such as fluconazole and pomelo, and those that lower levels, such as rifampicin.', desc='Anything that blocks CYP3A or P-glycoprotein raises tacrolimus; anything that induces them lowers it — rifampicin most dangerously of all.', abbrevs=[('TB', 'Tuberculosis'), ('CYP3A', 'Cytochrome P450 3A'), ('P-gp', 'P-glycoprotein')]),
    dict(id='TN06-F5', mid='06', section='reading', stem='transplant-nurse-06-reading-a-run-of-levels', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='One level is a snapshot; a run of levels is a story', content='Worked example of five weekly tacrolimus troughs, each annotated with the hidden fact that explains it (timing error, diarrhea, generic switch)', alt='Chart of five weeks of tacrolimus trough levels, each annotated: a reliable baseline, a falsely high peak drawn after the dose, a high level during diarrhea, and a low level after a generic switch.', desc='Behind every surprising level is usually a fact — timing, illness, a new product — that the nurse can collect before the prescriber reads the number.', abbrevs=[]),
    dict(id='TN07-F2', mid='07', section='rejection', stem='transplant-nurse-07-tcmr-vs-abmr', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='Two ways to reject a kidney', content='T-cell-mediated versus antibody-mediated rejection: mechanism, biopsy appearance and treatment approach', alt='Side-by-side comparison of T-cell-mediated rejection, with T cells invading tubules, and antibody-mediated rejection, with donor-specific antibodies inflaming small vessels and C4d staining, each with its biopsy appearance and treatment.', desc="T-cell rejection attacks tubules and usually responds to steroids; antibody rejection inflames the graft's small vessels and is harder to treat.", abbrevs=[('ATG', 'Anti-thymocyte globulin'), ('TCMR', 'T-cell-mediated rejection'), ('ABMR', 'Antibody-mediated rejection'), ('IVIG', 'Intravenous immunoglobulin'), ('PAS', 'Periodic acid–Schiff')]),
    dict(id='TN07-F3', mid='07', section='proteinuria', stem='transplant-nurse-07-glomerular-barrier-proteinuria', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='Protein in the urine: where the filter fails', content='The three-layer glomerular filtration barrier and the three common causes of graft proteinuria, with correct urine sampling', alt='Magnified glomerular capillary wall showing its three layers holding back albumin, a damaged section leaking protein, and three common causes of proteinuria after kidney transplant with sampling tips.', desc='Creatinine shows how much the graft filters; protein in the urine shows how well its three-layer filter holds together.', abbrevs=[('mTOR', 'Mechanistic target of rapamycin'), ('UPCR', 'Urine protein-to-creatinine ratio'), ('UACR', 'Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio')]),
    dict(id='TN07-F4', mid='07', section='biopsy', stem='transplant-nurse-07-graft-biopsy-safety', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='Transplant kidney biopsy: before, during, after', content='Ultrasound-guided percutaneous biopsy of a transplant kidney in the right iliac fossa, before/after nursing checks, and pooled complication rates', alt='Supine patient undergoing ultrasound-guided biopsy of a transplant kidney in the right lower abdomen, with an ultrasound inset, before-and-after nursing checklists and pooled complication rates.', desc='Graft biopsy is safe in most patients; careful pre-procedure checks and close post-biopsy observation catch the uncommon bleeding complications early.', abbrevs=[('BP', 'Blood pressure')]),
    dict(id='TN08-F2', mid='08', section='signs', stem='transplant-nurse-08-masked-infection-signs', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='Infection after transplant: quieter signs, same danger', content="How immunosuppression blunts the inflammatory signs of infection, with what to look for instead, and the 'net state of immunosuppression'", alt='Four-row card comparing the usual signs of infection with the quieter signs seen after kidney transplant, beside a stack of factors that make up the net state of immunosuppression.', desc="The drugs that protect the graft also hide the signs of infection; look for subtle changes and add up everything that weakens the recipient's defenses.", abbrevs=[('CMV', 'Cytomegalovirus'), ('SpO₂', 'Oxygen saturation')]),
    dict(id='TN08-F3', mid='08', section='cmv', stem='transplant-nurse-08-cmv-risk-matrix-prevention', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='CMV: who is at risk, and two ways to prevent it', content='CMV risk by donor/recipient serostatus and the two prevention strategies (universal prophylaxis vs preemptive monitoring), with the letermovir trial result', alt='Serostatus matrix showing CMV risk highest in donor-positive, recipient-negative recipients, beside cards comparing universal prophylaxis and preemptive monitoring, and the letermovir trial result.', desc='Donor and recipient CMV serostatus sets the risk; programs prevent disease either by giving every at-risk recipient an antiviral or by testing often and treating early.', abbrevs=[('CMV', 'Cytomegalovirus')]),
    dict(id='TN08-F4', mid='08', section='bk', stem='transplant-nurse-08-bk-screening-schedule-thresholds', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='BK virus: screen because it is silent', content='Silent progression of BK polyomavirus from urine to blood to nephropathy, the 2024 consensus screening schedule, and the action thresholds', alt='BK polyomavirus progression from urine to blood to graft nephropathy without symptoms, a screening calendar of monthly blood tests to month 9 then every 3 months to 2 years, and thresholds for reducing immunosuppression.', desc='BK virus injures the graft silently, so recipients are screened on a schedule; rising viral loads prompt the team to reduce immunosuppression.', abbrevs=[('IVIG', 'Intravenous immunoglobulin'), ('BK', 'BK polyomavirus')]),
    dict(id='TN09-F2', mid='09', section='longterm', stem='transplant-nurse-09-long-term-risk-map', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='After year one: the long game', content='Long-term threats after the first year — cardiovascular disease, post-transplant diabetes, cancer, bone disease, chronic graft injury — linked to their drivers across organ systems', alt='Clothed transplant recipient surrounded by five long-term risk zones — heart disease, post-transplant diabetes, skin and other cancers, bone loss and chronic graft injury — with their drivers and screening points.', desc='After the first year, the biggest threats are cardiovascular disease, diabetes, cancer and bone loss — many driven by the same drugs that protect the graft.', abbrevs=[('BP', 'Blood pressure')]),
    dict(id='TN09-F3', mid='09', section='followup', stem='transplant-nurse-09-follow-up-taper-closed-loop', scaffold='D', size='1672 × 941', title='Follow-up tapers; every result still needs a closed loop', content='KDIGO-suggested creatinine monitoring schedule tapering over the first year, and the four steps that close the loop on every result', alt='Tapering follow-up timeline from daily creatinine in week one to every two to three months after the first year, above a four-step loop for every result: received, reviewed, action documented and patient told.', desc='Monitoring is most frequent when rejection, toxicity and infection are most likely; whatever the frequency, a result is finished only when its loop is closed.', abbrevs=[]),
